---
import JapanServicePage from "@components/japan/JapanServicePage.astro";
import type { Article } from "@components/japan/JapanServicePage.astro";
import { createPageSeo } from "@lib/seo/seo-config";
import { createBreadcrumbs } from "@lib/breadcrumbs/breadcrumbs";
import { usd, countryOgImage } from "@data/countries/international";
import { JPY_PER_USD, jpy, both, ja, japanRegions, japanServiceSchema } from "@data/countries/japan";

const path = "/japan/website-developer/";

// ---------- FAQ (visible list and FAQPage schema share this array; plain text only) ----------

const faqs = [
  {
    question: "How much does it cost to have a website made in Japan?",
    answer: `According to 2026 price guides from Japanese agencies and comparison sites, a corporate site of around five pages typically costs ¥150,000 to ¥650,000, about 30 pages costs ¥300,000 to ¥1,250,000, and a 10-page WordPress site is around ¥800,000. Our static websites start from ${both("Static")}, SEO websites of 700+ pages from ${both("SEO website")} and ecommerce storefronts from ${both("Ecommerce")}. Each is a starting price for a first version with an agreed scope. Your written quote depends on page count, features, languages and integrations, and third-party costs such as the domain and hosting are paid by you directly.`,
  },
  {
    question: "Why is your website price lower than a Japanese web production company?",
    answer: "A Japanese web production company carries Japanese costs: office rent, salaried directors and sales staff, and often a chain of subcontractors that each add a margin. We are three engineers in India who work remotely, with no office, no sales team and no subcontractors, so our costs are much lower. The trade-offs are real and we state them upfront: we work in English, we do not write your Japanese copy, and we cannot meet you in person or photograph your premises.",
  },
  {
    question: "Do you have an office or staff in Japan?",
    answer: "No. BtechWaleTech has no office, registered company, staff or address in Japan. We are three engineers based in India: Ankur Kumar builds front ends, back ends and integrations, Santosh Sharma handles AI, AWS, data, technical SEO and deployment, and Vedansh Shrivastava runs project delivery. You message and talk to them directly, with no account manager in between. All communication is remote, by WhatsApp, email and video call, and you can check progress whenever you like on a private staging site.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can you write the Japanese text for our website?",
    answer: "No, and it is worth being careful with any overseas developer who says yes. Our team works in English and does not write natural Japanese. We build the Japanese site correctly, with Japanese fonts, line breaking, furigana fields, postal code lookup and proper language attributes, and the words come from you, a bilingual colleague or a professional translator or copywriter. We keep all site text in one shared sheet so your writer can work without touching code, and we import it exactly as approved.",
  },
  {
    question: "How long does it take to build a corporate website?",
    answer: "A static corporate site usually takes one to two weeks of build time once the content is ready, and an SEO website of 700+ pages takes three to five weeks. What stretches a Japanese project is rarely the code: it is waiting for Japanese copy, photos and approval from several people inside the company, sometimes including the president. We agree dates for content and feedback in the scope, and we show progress on a staging link throughout, so nothing waits for a big reveal at the end.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can you renew our existing website without losing Google rankings?",
    answer: "Yes, if the renewal is planned around your URLs. Before any design work we crawl the current site, export the pages that receive search traffic or backlinks, and map every old URL to its new equivalent. On launch day we set permanent 301 redirects, update internal links, submit a new sitemap and then watch indexing and 404 errors for the following weeks. Most traffic lost in a site renewal comes from missing redirects and deleted content, not from the new design. Nobody can promise rankings, but we remove the avoidable risks.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can you move our site from Wix, STUDIO, Jimdo or an old WordPress theme?",
    answer: "Yes. Hosted builders such as Wix, Jimdo and STUDIO keep the design inside their platforms, so a move normally means rebuilding the design on the new stack and transferring the text, images and news posts. Old WordPress sites can be moved more directly, although outdated themes and plugins are often better replaced than patched. In every case we list the old URLs first and redirect them, keep your domain in your company's name, and switch DNS only after you approve the new site on staging.",
  },
  {
    question: "Should our website use WordPress or be a static site?",
    answer: "It depends on who edits the site and how often. If your staff publish news, columns or job openings every week, WordPress or a headless CMS makes sense, provided someone keeps plugins and PHP updated. If the site changes a few times a month, a static site is faster, cheaper to host and much harder to hack, and we can still add a simple editing screen for news. We recommend one after hearing how your team works, and we explain the running costs of each option before you decide.",
  },
  {
    question: "Do your forms support furigana, postal code lookup and full-width input?",
    answer: "Yes. Our Japanese forms put the family name first, include a furigana field, fill in the prefecture and city from a seven-digit postal code, and accept full-width numbers and letters by converting them instead of showing an error. Phone numbers work with or without hyphens. Where your audience expects it, we add a confirmation screen before sending, clear required labels, error messages next to each field and spam protection. Submissions go to email and, if you like, to Chatwork, Slack or Microsoft Teams.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can you build a bilingual Japanese and English website?",
    answer: "Yes, and it is one of the most useful things we do for foreign-owned companies, exporters and inbound tourism businesses. Every language lives in its own folder, for example /en/ beside /ja/, connected by hreflang tags so Google serves the right version; the language switcher opens the matching page, and each language gets suitable fonts. We never force visitors to another language by IP address. Chinese and Korean versions follow the same pattern. Translations come from native translators; we handle the structure, forms and publishing.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can you invoice in yen, and how do we pay?",
    answer: `Yes. Our prices are published in US dollars, with yen figures at about ¥${JPY_PER_USD} per dollar for comparison; in September 2026 the rate moved between about ¥153 and ¥157. When your accounts team would rather pay in yen, we fix a JPY amount in the written quote, and currency swings during the build leave it unchanged. You pay by Wise or international bank transfer in stages: part in advance, part on design approval and the rest before the site goes live.`,
  },
  {
    question: "Do your invoices include Japanese consumption tax?",
    answer: "No. Our company is based in India and has not registered in Japan as a qualified invoice issuer. Our invoices therefore carry no Japanese consumption tax and do not count as qualified invoices under the system Japan introduced in October 2023. The consumption tax treatment of a service bought from abroad, and any withholding question, depend on your company's situation, so your tax accountant (zeirishi) should make that call. We are happy to supply our company details and clear, itemised invoices to support that review.",
  },
  {
    question: "Who owns the website, the domain and the code?",
    answer: "Your company does. The domain, hosting or cloud account, analytics, Search Console and any CMS or Shopify account are registered in your company's name, or transferred to it before launch, and you receive every login. The source code is handed over, usually in a Git repository you control. We do not hold sites hostage for renewal fees. Fonts, images and plugins are properly licensed, and paid licences are bought in your name so they stay valid if you ever change developer.",
  },
  {
    question: "Where will our website be hosted, and can we use a .co.jp domain?",
    answer: "Static sites are usually served from a global CDN with edge servers in Japan, which keeps pages fast from Hokkaido to Okinawa. WordPress sites run well on Japanese hosts such as Xserver or Sakura Internet, and larger sites can use AWS, Google Cloud or Azure regions in Tokyo and Osaka. Registering .jp requires a Japanese postal address, and .co.jp is reserved for companies registered in Japan, with one per company, so the registration is done by your company; we then connect DNS, SSL and email records.",
  },
  {
    question: "What are your working hours in Japan time?",
    answer: "We are online from 12:30 pm to 12:30 am Japan time, which is 9 am to 9 pm in India, every day of the week on WhatsApp. The gap between the two countries is a fixed three and a half hours, because neither uses daylight saving time, so the schedule is the same in January and July. Feedback you send in the morning is waiting for us when we start, and most changes appear on the staging link before your evening. Calls around 10 am Japan time can be arranged in advance.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can you build a recruitment site for our company?",
    answer: `Yes. A recruitment site usually has a message from the company, job descriptions with working conditions, staff interviews, a day-in-the-life page, photos of the real workplace, an FAQ and an application or casual-interview form that works on a phone. JobPosting structured data makes your openings eligible for Google's job listings in search, and we can link to ads you already run on job boards. Recruitment sites can share the corporate site's design system, and a static build starts from ${both("Static")}.`,
  },
  {
    question: "Will the website meet Japanese accessibility standards?",
    answer: "We build to WCAG 2.2 level AA as a working target. JIS X 8341-3, Japan's web accessibility standard, is aligned with WCAG, and its 2016 edition uses the WCAG 2.0 success criteria, so a site built this way is well placed for a JIS conformance check. In practice that means readable contrast, keyboard access, visible focus, labelled form fields, meaningful alt text and headings, and text that can be enlarged without breaking layouts. Since April 2024, private businesses in Japan must provide reasonable accommodation for people with disabilities. For a formal test report, a specialist tester can audit the finished site.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can our website be paid for with an IT subsidy?",
    answer: "Not with IT adoption subsidies. Programmes such as the IT Introduction Subsidy and its successors require registered IT support providers and registered tools, and we are not registered, so our work is not subsidy-eligible. Other small business subsidies sometimes cover website or sales-promotion costs under strict conditions, but whether an overseas vendor qualifies depends on each programme's rules. Check with the programme office or your local chamber of commerce before choosing any vendor. If subsidy funding is essential, a registered Japanese provider is the safer choice.",
  },
  {
    question: "What should we prepare before contacting a website developer?",
    answer: "A short list is enough to start: what the business does and for whom, the pages you think you need, three to five websites you like and why, your logo files, and who approves decisions. If you are renewing a site, give us access to Google Search Console and analytics so we can protect the pages that bring traffic. Photos you own, Japanese text or a translator, and access to your domain registrar speed things up. Missing items are fine; we list them in the scope with dates.",
  },
  {
    question: "What happens after the website launches?",
    answer: `You receive five months of maintenance free after launch: text and image updates, bug fixes, CMS and plugin updates where relevant, backups and speed checks. From the sixth month you can keep a maintenance plan from ${both("Maintenance")}, or simply message us whenever a change is needed. Japanese maintenance plans that include content updates are commonly quoted at ¥20,000 to ¥50,000 a month, so compare what each plan includes, not only the monthly figure. Hosting, domain and platform fees are paid by you directly to those providers.`,
  },
  {
    question: "Do you do SEO as part of the website build?",
    answer: `Every build includes technical SEO basics: unique titles and descriptions, a clean heading structure, structured data, an XML sitemap, canonical tags, fast pages and redirects from old URLs. Ongoing SEO, such as Japanese keyword research with a native writer, content planning and monthly Search Console reports, is a separate plan from ${both("Monthly SEO")}, against about ¥100,000 to ¥300,000 a month for SME SEO consulting in Japan. Yahoo! JAPAN uses Google's search technology, so one strategy covers both. We do not guarantee rankings, because no one can.`,
  },
  {
    question: "Can you build a Shopify store for selling in Japan?",
    answer: `Yes. Japanese agencies typically charge ¥300,000 to ¥1,000,000 for a basic Shopify build, and moving from another cart often adds 20 to 50 percent; our storefronts start from ${both("Ecommerce")}. We set up the theme, collections, delivery date options, tax-inclusive prices, the Specified Commercial Transactions Act page and the payment methods Japanese shoppers expect, such as cards including JCB, konbini payment and bank transfer, through a payment provider approved in your company's name. Shopify plan fees, apps and payment fees are paid by you directly.`,
  },
];

// ---------- Page meta ----------

const page = createPageSeo({
