  {
    id: "core-web-vitals-fonts",
    heading: "Fast Japanese websites: Core Web Vitals and Japanese fonts",
    paragraphs: [
      "Speed problems on Japanese sites usually start with fonts and images. A Latin font holds a few hundred characters; a Japanese font covers thousands of kanji, kana and symbols, so a complete web font file can run to several megabytes. Load two weights of Noto Sans JP carelessly and the text may appear late or jump when the font arrives, which hurts Google's Core Web Vitals and, more importantly, the visitor.",
      "Google measures three things: Largest Contentful Paint (how quickly the main content appears, good at 2.5 seconds or less), Interaction to Next Paint (how quickly the page responds to a tap, good at 200 milliseconds or less) and Cumulative Layout Shift (how much the layout jumps, good at 0.1 or less). We check them in real-user field data in Search Console and PageSpeed Insights, not only in a lab test on a fast office connection.",
    ],
    list: [
      "<strong>System fonts first.</strong> Hiragino Sans on iPhone and Mac and Yu Gothic or Meiryo on Windows are already installed and look good. For many corporate sites they are the right choice and cost zero bytes.",
      "<strong>Subset web fonts when the brand needs them.</strong> Google Fonts delivers Japanese fonts in small slices split by Unicode range, so browsers download only the characters a page uses. For self-hosted brand fonts we create subsets for headings, which use few distinct characters, and keep system fonts for body text.",
      "<strong>Fallbacks tuned to avoid jumps.</strong> We set font-display and match fallback font metrics, so the switch from a system font to the web font does not shift the layout.",
      "<strong>Text, not pictures of text.</strong> Japanese banners and landing pages often put headlines inside images. Real text is lighter, sharper on high-density screens, readable by search engines and screen readers, and editable by your staff.",
