  {
    id: "website-renewal",
    heading: "Website renewal without losing rankings: URL mapping, redirects and migrations",
    paragraphs: [
      `In Japan a website redesign is called a renewal (${ja("リニューアル")}), and many companies go through one every few years. The most common way a renewal goes wrong is not the design. It is changing URLs without redirects, dropping pages that quietly brought in traffic, or launching with a staging setting that tells search engines not to index the site. All three are avoidable with a disciplined checklist.`,
    ],
    list: [
      "<strong>Inventory before design.</strong> We crawl the current site and export the pages with clicks and impressions from Search Console, plus pages that other sites link to. Pages that bring visitors are kept, merged or improved, never deleted by accident.",
      "<strong>A redirect map.</strong> A spreadsheet listing every old URL and its new URL, including old WordPress addresses such as ?p=123, category and tag archives, PDF brochures and images that other sites link to, and URLs generated by site builders.",
      "<strong>Permanent 301 redirects.</strong> Each old URL redirects in one step to the most relevant new page, not to the home page, and chains of redirects from earlier renewals are flattened.",
      "<strong>Content parity.</strong> Titles, headings and main text on important pages move across without losing the words people searched for, and structured data is kept or added.",
      "<strong>Launch day checks.</strong> Remove noindex tags and staging passwords, submit the new XML sitemap, test redirects in bulk, and check canonical tags, forms, analytics and Search Console verification.",
      "<strong>The weeks after launch.</strong> We watch 404 errors, indexing and traffic for key pages and fix gaps quickly. Redirects stay in place long term; we recommend keeping them permanently where the host allows.",
    ],
    after: [
      "<strong>Moving from Wix, Jimdo or STUDIO.</strong> Hosted builders generally do not hand over a design you can install elsewhere, so the design is rebuilt and the content moved page by page, or by export where the platform allows it. Builder URLs often follow the platform's own patterns, and blog posts may sit under paths the new site will not reproduce, so the redirect map matters even more. Your domain stays with you; we point it to the new host only after you approve the site on staging, which keeps any interruption short.",
      "<strong>Moving from an old WordPress theme.</strong> Old themes built with heavy page builders and dozens of plugins are often slower and harder to secure than a fresh build. We export posts, pages and media, clean up the HTML, and either rebuild on a lean WordPress theme or move the content into a static site with a headless CMS. Shortcodes left over from old plugins are converted, not left on the page as broken text.",
      "<strong>Changing domain at the same time.</strong> If you move from an old domain to a new .co.jp or .com, Search Console's change of address tool helps Google understand the move once redirects are in place. Even so, we advise changing as little as possible at once, because a new design, new URLs and a new domain together make any problem harder to diagnose.",
