  {
    id: "bilingual-architecture",
    heading: "Bilingual Japanese and English website architecture",
    paragraphs: [
      "Many businesses in Japan must serve two audiences well at once: Japanese customers and staff, and overseas buyers, head offices, investors or visitors. Visitor numbers make the case: JNTO recorded 42.68 million international arrivals in 2025, a new high, against 36.87 million in 2024, and the three biggest source markets were South Korea, mainland China and Taiwan. For hospitality and retail, the second language is therefore often Korean or Chinese rather than English. Getting the structure right at the start costs far less than retrofitting it later.",
    ],
    list: [
      "<strong>One domain, language folders.</strong> We usually recommend paths such as /ja/ and /en/, or Japanese at the root and English under /en/, on one domain, so the authority each language earns helps the other. Separate domains make sense only for genuinely separate businesses or markets.",
      "<strong>hreflang and x-default.</strong> Each page declares its language versions with hreflang tags, including zh-Hans, zh-Hant and ko where used, plus an x-default for visitors who match none, so Google shows the right version in each market.",
      "<strong>No forced redirects by location.</strong> Visitors and search engine crawlers must be able to reach every language. A banner suggesting the other language is fine; an automatic redirect based on IP address is not.",
